  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_gcn_121",
      "name": "rate-limiting-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Read the rate limiter incidents",
      "description": "Three statements about the limits Rice Rocket put in front of its api. Decide whether each one is true.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "traffic-control"
      ],
      "points": 10,
      "hints": [
        "A token bucket refills over time but has a size. What happens to tokens that would overflow it?",
        "Who writes each entry of X-Forwarded-For? The client can send the header too, and proxies append to it.",
        "Each gateway pod keeps its own buckets in memory. How many pods can a client's requests land on?"
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The restaurant partner API is limited to `rate = 5` tokens per second with `burst = 10`. A partner's batch job sleeps overnight, then starts:\n\n```text\n02:00:00  last request\n07:00:00  job starts, sends 2,000 requests as fast as it can\n```\n\nStatement: \"After 5 idle hours the bucket holds 90,000 tokens, so the first 2,000 requests all pass at once.\"",
          "explanation": "False. A token bucket never holds more than its burst: refill is `min(burst, tokens + elapsed x rate)`. After any idle time the job gets 10 requests at once, then 5 per second. That cap is what makes the burst a safety limit; without it an idle client could flatten the api. The misconception is that a limiter banks unused capacity.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Requests come through the cloud load balancer (10.0.0.0/8), which appends the address it saw. The limiter keyed anonymous clients like this:\n\n```go\nhops := strings.Split(r.Header.Get(\"X-Forwarded-For\"), \",\")\nkey := strings.TrimSpace(hops[0])\n```\n\n```text\nX-Forwarded-For: 1.1.1.1, 203.0.113.66\nX-Forwarded-For: 2.2.2.2, 203.0.113.66\nX-Forwarded-For: 3.3.3.3, 203.0.113.66\n```\n\nStatement: \"The leftmost entry is safe to use here, because the load balancer is the one that sets X-Forwarded-For.\"",
          "explanation": "False. The load balancer appends to whatever the client sent, so everything left of the last entry is client-controlled. A scraper that sends a new fake address each time gets a fresh bucket each time, as in the log (the real address 203.0.113.66 is the last entry). Trust X-Forwarded-For only when the peer is your load balancer, and take the entry it added (the rightmost, with one proxy layer).",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The gateway runs 4 replicas behind the load balancer, each with an in-memory limiter of 10 requests per second per API key. A partner reports:\n\n```text\npartner key rest-12: sent 38 req/s for a minute, received 38 x 200, 0 x 429\n```\n\nStatement: \"Each pod counts only the requests it receives, so with requests spread over 4 pods a client can get up to about 4 times the configured rate; divide the limit by the replica count or keep the buckets in a shared store like Redis.\"",
          "explanation": "True. In-memory buckets are per process. Spread over 4 pods, 38 req/s is about 9.5 per pod, under each pod's limit of 10. Either set the per-pod limit to limit / replicas (simple, but uneven with sticky clients and wrong while the HPA changes the count), or move the counters to a shared store (Redis with an atomic script) when the limit matters. The misconception is that a per-process limiter enforces a global limit.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
